A window always has a colour space, and answering nil is a null nobody can trace.

-[NSWindow colorSpace] was NSUnimplementedMethod() returning nil. A nil colour space becomes a NULL
CGColorSpaceRef, and everything built from one, a bitmap context most of all, comes back NULL too;
the dereference then lands far from here with nothing connecting it back to a window accessor.

sRGB rather than a device space, because that is what a window on an ordinary display reports and
what this compositor is handed.

Found while tracing a CMake crash (#234), where it changed the failure from a null dereference into
a missing-symbol abort, which is how the rest of that symbol gap became visible at all.

--- a/AppKit/NSWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindow.m
@@ -2025,9 +2025,16 @@
     return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A WINDOW ALWAYS HAS A COLOUR SPACE. Answering nil hands the caller a NULL CGColorSpaceRef, and
+ * everything built from one, a bitmap context most of all, then comes back NULL too; the
+ * dereference lands far from here with nothing to connect it back. Task #234.
+ *
+ * sRGB rather than a device space, because that is what a window on an ordinary display reports and
+ * what the compositor is given.
+ */
 - (NSColorSpace *) colorSpace {
-    NSUnimplementedMethod();
-    return nil;
+    return [NSColorSpace sRGBColorSpace];
 }
 
 - (void) setColorSpace: (NSColorSpace *) newColorSpace {
